{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful and influential figure in the 12th century Crusader states. He ruled as Count of Tripoli from 1183 to 1203 a period marked by significant territorial expansion military campaigns and political maneuvering. His reign is considered a high point for the County of Tripoli establishing it as a major power in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was born in 1155 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Countess Ermengarde of Montfort. He inherited the County upon his father’s death in 1183. His early years were characterized by consolidating the existing territories defending against Muslim incursions and navigating complex alliances with other Crusader powers. He proved to be a capable military leader quickly gaining renown for his strategic skills and decisive victories. He fostered strong ties with the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick Barbarossa who provided crucial military support.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw significant territorial growth. He expanded the County's holdings through successful campaigns against the Ayyubid dynasty and other Muslim factions. He gained control of the coastal regions of Syria and further strengthened Tripoli's position as a vital trading hub. He established a strong administrative system and promoted economic development fostering trade and agriculture. His rule was also marked by a commitment to religious piety and the construction of numerous churches and monasteries.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III’s reign was dominated by numerous military campaigns aimed at expanding and defending Tripoli’s territories. He engaged in several major battles against the Ayyubids particularly in Syria. His victories secured control over key strategic locations and solidified Tripoli's position as a major military power. He also fought against other Crusader states and Muslim forces in the Levant. His military successes earned him considerable prestige and influence among the Crusader lords. He faced challenges from internal dissent and rivalries with other Crusader entities. He effectively managed these challenges through diplomacy and strategic alliances.\n\n*Decline and Death* Raymond III’s reign eventually came to an end in 1203. He died in battle during a campaign against the Ayyubids in Syria. His death marked the beginning of a period of decline for the County of Tripoli. Internal strife succession disputes and external pressures weakened the County's position. While Raymond III left a legacy of strength and territorial expansion his successors struggled to maintain his level of power and influence. His reign remains a pivotal period in the history of the Crusader states representing a golden age of Tripoli’s power and prestige. He is remembered as a brilliant military leader a shrewd politician and a devout Christian who played a significant role in shaping the history of the Levant.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1805190145969391}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.8874615458771586e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.07509470731019974}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.02148974873125553}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5968805551528931}, {"token": "▁and", "logprob": -1.6877937316894531}, {"token": "▁influential", "logprob": -0.02214827761054039}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -0.8164176940917969}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.029762335121631622}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0016617074143141508}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.615339457988739}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0012196014868095517}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁century", "logprob": -0.02432500757277012}, {"token": 